  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nFast moving upper flow across the northern tier of the CONUS with \nPacific NW shortwave energy ejecting into the upper Midwest, as a \ntransitory upper ridge amplifies over the Great Lakes.  This will \nresult in northward push of warm, moist air into our area tonight \ninto Tuesday. The shortwave will shear out as it pushes toward James \nBay, dragging a cold front across the area. This boundary will stall \nout to our south for the mid to late week period, with additional \nshortwaves bringing precipitation chances, focused mainly to our \nsouth.  \n\nMoisture increases quickly tonight along the slow moving frontal \nboundary to our west.  The best forcing, upper divergence and \ndeepest moisture puts the heaviest rain potential from upper \nMichigan southwest into parts of Wisconsin. Full saturation on \nforecast soundings becomes a question mark as you head further \nsouth, as they show a wedge of drier air below 850mb. The heaviest \nrain in our area will likely fall over eastern upper Michigan and \nthe Tip of the Mitt, where totals of 0.50\" to 1.00\" will be \npossible. Further south, rainfall amounts will drop off, with some \nareas only seeing 0.25\" to 0.50\". Tuesday will start wet, with \ndiminishing showers as the day progresses, as drier air begins \nto push into the area from the west. It will be breezy, as we \npartially mix down 35kt winds at the top of the boundary layer. \nExpect wind gusts of 20-30 mph over interior areas with some \nisolated 40+ mph gusts possible over coastal areas. \n\nFor the middle/end of the week, additional shortwave energy will \nride along that quasi-stationary boundary to our south.  This will \nkeep most of the precipitation chances south of us, with lesser \nshower chances over northern Michigan.  There are some signals for a \npotentially stronger shortwave to eject out of the western trough \nand move toward the Great Lakes over the weekend or early next week, \nwhich could bring better chances of rain. Temperatures fairly close \nto normal for mid September, with highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s \nand lows in the 40s to mid 50s.",
